/**
 * @defgroup buffer Owned Buffers
 * @ingroup module_data_structures
 * @brief Bounded aligned storage, explicit ownership and worker workspaces.
 */
/**
 * @page topic_buffer Owned Buffers
 * @ingroup buffer
 *
 * Payload pointers returned by buffer_alloc() are ownership tokens. Their
 * capacity, logical length, alignment and owning domain are stored privately.
 * buffer_release(pointer) releases one reference and clears the typed pointer.
 * Only the owning pointer may be queried or released: interior pointers and
 * views into stack, codec or external storage remain borrowed.
 *
 * @section buffer_example Ownership
 * @code{.c}
 * void *payload = buffer_alloc(NULL, 4096);
 * if (!payload)
 *   return SET_ERRNO(ERROR_MEMORY, "Payload budget exhausted");
 * // Fill payload before publication.
 * asciichat_error_t result = packet_queue_push_take(queue, PACKET_TYPE_PING,
 *                                                  &payload, 4096, 0);
 * // Success clears payload. Failure leaves it here for retry or release.
 * buffer_release(payload);
 * @endcode
 *
 * buffer_share() creates an additional immutable reference. Every recipient
 * releases its own reference. Mutation, including changing logical length,
 * requires exclusive ownership. Reference counting does not synchronize access
 * to payload contents. A transport retains ownership until its actual completion
 * contract permits release; caching memory does not extend a borrowed lifetime.
 *
 * @section buffer_domains Domains and limits
 * buffer_domain_create() supplies an isolated capacity-class cache and budget.
 * A mutex serializes allocation, return and trimming. There is no concurrent
 * free-list reclamation, ABA-sensitive stack, or allocator-origin guessing.
 * Backing allocations use the existing tracked allocation machinery. Manually
 * aligning within that allocation means Windows uses the matching ordinary
 * deallocator, without mixing aligned_malloc and free.
 *
 * Domain limits include backing capacity, payload headers and alignment padding.
 * Defaults allow 512 MiB process-wide, reserving 8 MiB for control traffic.
 * The default media domain allows 337 MiB with at most 32 MiB cached. Queue
 * domains allow 64 MiB with at most 8 MiB cached, in addition to queue byte limits.
 * These are bounded storage budgets, not a guarantee that every request succeeds.
 * The process budget also accounts for domain objects, including closed domains
 * retained by outstanding buffers. Oversized blocks are budgeted but not cached.
 * Exhaustion returns ERROR_MEMORY; no heap fallback bypasses the limit.
 * Control traffic has a bounded reserved allowance through buffer_control_domain().
 * This budget covers this API's storage, not the entire process RSS or third-party
 * codec allocations. Cache trim returns allocations to the backing allocator;
 * it does not promise that the OS immediately reduces RSS.
 *
 * buffer_domain_close() stops admissions and frees cached storage. Join users of
 * the domain pointer first. Outstanding payloads keep the domain alive and may
 * be released afterwards, including by another thread. The last release reclaims
 * a closed domain. Application startup/shutdown owns the default domains.
 *
 * @section buffer_workspaces Repeated work
 * Zero-initialize buffer_workspace_t. buffer_workspace_reserve() retains capacity
 * across frames and preserves existing storage on failure. Destroy the workspace
 * when its worker stops. Compression workspaces retain the zstd context too.
 * Frame-send workspaces retain compression and packet assembly buffers; render
 * file contexts retain pixel-copy storage. Audio prepares storage when streams
 * or mixer membership change, outside the sample mixing loop.
 *
 * @section buffer_validation Validation
 * Build test-buffer-ownership and run ctest -R '^buffer-ownership$'. This native
 * suite runs on Windows, Linux and macOS without Criterion. It tests alignment,
 * size-class reuse, overflow, exhaustion, immutable sharing, failure preservation,
 * shutdown with outstanding owners, concurrent trimming, MPMC ownership transfer,
 * process-wide exhaustion with control reserve, queues and media storage.
 * ASan builds poison cached payloads to expose access after the final release.
 */
